      "evidence_type": "published tag insertion at this exact site",
      "functional_impact_measured": "Flow cytometry and confocal microscopy confirmed surface expression of HA-KCNH2 in HEK293T cells (L9). Multiple independent studies using HA tags in the same S1\u2013S2 loop explicitly state the tag 'does not interfere with channel processing or function' (L11, L14). Explicit electrophysiology of the T443/E444 junction construct specifically was not reported in L7.",
      "confidence": "high",
      "rationale": "L7 (DOI:10.1101/2020.02.17.952606) explicitly names the junction T443/E444 in full-length KCNH2, a distinct position from the T436/E437 BBS site. Python verification confirms aa(443)=T and aa(444)=E. The junction is 14 residues from TM1 end (429) and 10 residues from TM2 start (454). Although C445 is only 1 residue downstream of E444, the published insertion was tolerated and the channel trafficked to the surface; L11 and L14 from independent groups confirm HA tags in this same loop do not perturb processing or function. Full-length KV11.1 (KCNH2) expressed in HEK293T cells; a 9-amino-acid HA tag with flanking 4-amino-acid linkers (NSEHYPYDVPDYAVTFE, 17 aa total) was inserted between residues T443 and E444 in the extracellular S1\u2013S2 loop by inverse PCR. Surface expression of HA-KCNH2 was validated by flow cytometry and confocal microscopy; multiple control variants were generated in the same backbone. [validation: surface_and_function; position: validated; source: paper; entailment_verified: True]",
      "sources": [
        {
          "citation": "preprint",
          "claim": "A previously published 34 9 amino acid HA tag with surrounding 4 amino acid linkers (NSEHYPYDVPDYAVTFE) was inserted between amino acids T443 and E444 (in the extracellular region between the S1 and S2 helices) by inverse PCR 35 (Table S1)."
        }
